-- One type variable, bound from two positions written at two different refinements of one type.
--
-- [repr.md](doc/spec/repr.md#bit-width-refinements) makes `@bits(n)` invisible to everything that
-- decides a type: a refinement and what it refines are one type to dispatch, to overload resolution
-- and to inference. What a variable bound twice takes is therefore the *canonical* type - the
-- natural width, which is what a load already produces and what an operation on two refinements
-- already happens at. Only a store narrows again.
--
-- Written as a record because a record's type argument is inferred by the same solve a call's type
-- arguments are, and used to be the one place that answered differently: the variable took whichever
-- position was written first, so the two constructions below produced two different types. One of
-- them then held a `U64` in a field declared to hold 53 bits, and the other did not - and neither
-- the order nor the difference is anything the author wrote.
alias Id = @bits(53) U64

data Same(a) {left: a, right: a}

-- Nothing here says what `Same`'s type argument is: the expected type of a construction short-cuts
-- the inference, so both of these have to reach it on the arguments alone.
fn sum(both: Same(U64)) -> U64 = both.left + both.right

fn narrowFirst(narrow: Id, plain: U64) -> U64:
    let both = Same {left: narrow, right: plain}
    return sum(both)

fn plainFirst(plain: U64, narrow: Id) -> U64:
    let both = Same {left: plain, right: narrow}
    return sum(both)

fn main() -> Int = (truncate(narrowFirst(21, 4000)) :: Int) + (truncate(plainFirst(300, 5)) :: Int)
